  "decision": {
    "verdict": "ADOPT the sharded observer",
    "kind": "recorded falsification with an adoption",
    "summary": "The 11D brief predicted the single DSFB-observer mutex would become visible as independent requests advanced through search simultaneously under the 11E pool. The oracle shows the prediction was TRUE IN THE OBSERVER ROWS but NOT END-TO-END: the plan call (the largest critical section, a 9-element sort under the lock) lost ~66% of its wall under 16-way concurrency (34-38 ms -> 10.5-11.5 ms at the 4x scale; 3.3-20.1 ms -> 2.1-2.2 ms at the sealed scale), but all observer calls together are ~1 us each, 0.1% of prepare even at 4x scale, so wall, p50/p99, and useful CPU are unchanged within +/-1% run-to-run noise. The +2.6-3.7% useful-CPU delta the 11E probe measured for the pool is therefore NOT DSFB mutex wait (a mutex wait is wall, not CPU); it is the pool's higher effective parallelism.",
    "adoption_rationale": [
      "architecture: the observer's state is per-key, so its locking should be per-key; the write path is now synchronization-free end to end except the commit coordinator and the per-inode locks (real shared state, not advisory evidence)",
      "future-proofing: Phase-12C (DSFB structural semiotics) deepens the per-call work, which would make a single mutex matter",
      "zero measured regression in every row (byte identity exact, logical bytes == input exactly, candidates and families identical)"
    ],
